    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d94">

                <pb facs="0106" n="102" sameAs="frus:frus1939v03/pg102"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.9315 Manchuria/203: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Harbin</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Merrell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harbin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-12-30T15:00:00+08:00">December 30,
                            1939—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 31—4 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>32. My No. 31, December 28, 6 p.m.<anchor n="18" xml:id="d94-f1"/> Kubota, the
                    head of the Japanese delegation who has just returned from Chita, informs me
                    that a hot discussion was held and no conclusions reached at the conference. He
                    believes, however, that the Soviet and Mongol delegates are showing a
                    conciliatory spirit and that there is a possibility of success in Harbin.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking. Code text by air mail to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>[<hi rendition="#small-caps">Merrell</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
